#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532765} :activity/id #uuid "1ad6236a-078f-5f1f-ae8e-f7d60af63ee7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048771 :topic/id #uuid "bd590864-b974-5af1-ab3d-4de57a31118a" :topic/math-academy-id 311} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532634 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cad2650d-fc76-528a-9adf-020467802be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{407}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532370}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532370} :answer-field/id #uuid "25149fb3-c68d-59af-b172-4e169f2ea0c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "250e2076-51f3-53c1-af8d-a5206b051376" :question/math-academy-id "q-351774" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2cd4f059ef78b3ce7d6043dc216494340c08eb43edeab9b3869cd733c2f6d144.png)\n\nFind the total finite area $A$ enclosed between the curves $y=f(x)={x}^{3}-13x-10$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{3}-13x-10 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-13x-12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=-1$ is a root of ${x}^{3}-13x-12,$ and if we divide ${x}^{3}-13x-12$ by $x+1$ using synthetic division, we get ${x}^{2}-x-12.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,-1,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c4181d72f44f0190d03fa4239718e4f2877fda19a7b1f87421ef2746e27bcbc.png)\n\n  \n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $x\\in [-3,-1],$ and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(f(x)-g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(({x}^{3}-13x-10)-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}({x}^{3}-13x-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{x}^{2}-12x){|}_{-3}^{-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}(-1)^{2}-12(-1))-(\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}(-3)^{2}-12(-3)) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}+12)-(\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{117}{2}+36) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{4}-(-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $x\\in [-1,4],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{4}(g(x)-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{4}(2-({x}^{3}-13x-10))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{4}({x}^{3}-13x-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}{x}^{2}-12x){|}_{-1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{{4}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}({4}^{2})-12(4))+(\\frac{(-1)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}(-1)^{2}-12(-1)) \\\\  & =-(64-104-48)+(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{13}{2}+12) \\\\  & =-(-88)+(\\frac{23}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{375}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=8+\\frac{375}{4}=\\frac{407}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "15edc486-82c2-5128-8bf5-0227a35deb09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532735}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662ac86f-4640-5378-8ff2-bbb6d5c6c462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532160} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9f6404-d0d1-555c-b66d-86ac7d693415" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2b8242a3-946d-5b5e-b1f5-13dafc162222" :question/math-academy-id "q-351822" :question/problem "![](images/87/87ce042a004af61a206bcef79711935dffb1b00d7157989532914ed355c007e1.png)\n\nWhat is the total finite area $A$ enclosed between the curves $x=-2{y}^{1/3}$ and $x=-\\frac{y}{8},$ as shown above? Do not round the answer.\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(y)=-2{y}^{1/3}$ and $g(y)=-\\frac{y}{8}.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ -2{y}^{1/3} & =-\\frac{y}{8} \\\\ 16{y}^{1/3} & =y \\\\ 4096y & ={y}^{3} \\\\ y({y}^{2}-4096) & =0 \\\\ y(y-64)(y+64) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce1c5d7fe654b406671eebf60eb65af5e11e20bb83adc08d81f6afca14856228.png)\n\n \n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $y\\in [-64,0],$ and the right function is $f(y)$ while the left function is $g(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-64}^{0}(f(y)-g(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-64}^{0}(-2{y}^{1/3}-(-\\frac{y}{8}))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-64}^{0}(-2{y}^{1/3}+\\frac{y}{8})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{3{y}^{4/3}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})|}_{-64}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-384+256) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $y\\in [0,64],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}(-\\frac{y}{8}-(-2{y}^{1/3}))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}(-\\frac{y}{8}+2{y}^{1/3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{3{y}^{4/3}}{2})|}_{0}^{64} \\\\  & =(-256+384)-0 \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=128+128=256.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3751a9bd-d099-5d27-83e4-6f3b2b251fee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532740 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ee93b2-cfeb-50ed-aafc-620909d895c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531941} {:answer/id #uuid "181b08a8-e848-5de5-baed-41104e58e10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531981} {:answer/id #uuid "3224ae77-c3e6-58c2-a98f-a3a186e52051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532035} {:answer/id #uuid "57e7febd-cf7c-58da-b9bb-2214d53efc34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532126} {:answer/id #uuid "63047939-2514-5498-ae22-e5669ff0a0e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532126} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f2f1e55-4f59-572e-847b-dcb391381c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "78eccfd7-1b8e-5e65-93e9-1255565406bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-83553" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbb0eea61f7b4a4c6aa9f9b06462c327c9c6a366b5ff0ca21378f9be888f5ede.png)\n\nWhat is the total finite area enclosed between the curves $x=f(y)=3{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}$ and $x=g(y)=3y+6,$ as shown above?  \n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 3{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2} & =3y+6 \\\\ {y}^{3}+2{y}^{2} & =y+2 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y+2)-(y+2) & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa11be3a96d2055899bd17d700f1e59e7715bae2550d60d5c3a05d9f999b4008.png)\n\n \n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $y\\in [-2,-1],$ and the right function is $f(y)$ while the left function is $g(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[f(y)-g(y)]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(3{y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}-3y-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{4}+2{y}^{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}-6y)|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4}-2-\\frac{3}{2}+6)-(12-16-6+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $y\\in [-1,1],$ and the right function is $g(y)$ while the left function is $f(y).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(g(y)-f(y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3y+6-3{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+6y-\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{4}-2{y}^{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+6-\\frac{3}{4}-2)-(\\frac{3}{2}-6-\\frac{3}{4}+2) \\\\  & =(4+\\frac{3}{4})-(\\frac{3}{4}-4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{5}{4}+8=\\frac{37}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "58e50150-93f6-5924-840c-44b85199257a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532732}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2464e71d-94a2-53db-a635-1332ed3b3b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532010} :answer-field/id #uuid "340857ff-4276-54b0-8e43-52c97591e55f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ee79ba2d-b35e-5476-9b95-72d6c95d61d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-353285" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6cd07ff0c0f5b1b092e6501f41e9f61f3f98e794b5075461acfb1e97309cb32.png)\n\nFind the total finite area $A$ enclosed between the curves $y=15{x}^{4}+1$ and $y=15{x}^{2}+1,$ as shown above.  Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=15{x}^{4}+1$ and $g(x)=15{x}^{2}+1.$ First, we find the intersections of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(x)=g(x) \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}+1=15{x}^{2}+1 \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}=15{x}^{2} \\\\  & 15{x}^{4}-15{x}^{2}=0 \\\\  & 15{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/66/66ae463184e8d5fc9f667019be7fc5c893aea736a6d0dad8fa6a2619df9f08e8.png)\n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $x\\in [-1,0],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(g(x)-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}((15{x}^{2}+1)-(15{x}^{4}+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(15{x}^{2}-15{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{5})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(5(-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{5}) \\\\  & =0-(-5+3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $x\\in [0,1],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(g(x)-f(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((15{x}^{2}+1)-(15{x}^{4}+1))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{x}^{2}-15{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(5-3)-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db9f3360-dfbc-5056-bcf9-dcdc3020d117"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d10487d-2d9d-56d7-9cdc-96a964a7af60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{4}-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532023} {:answer/id #uuid "50f305d3-6119-5d45-a72e-f8145c2fdc0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{x}^{4}-x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532109} {:answer/id #uuid "55668ec2-5826-5d6a-8956-9daa48c55890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532118} {:answer/id #uuid "7745504f-0ea1-5f16-a5f1-5c985b944340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532200} {:answer/id #uuid "da418d57-2468-5fdc-87b4-fdc98be18a66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532401}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532118} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbc7a5d4-412e-593d-a67c-38cb29a7b665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "94f0b932-fd80-552e-9cee-96a821a87f03" :question/math-academy-id "q-50100" :question/problem "![](images/77/778cc96e20eead41160c37b8a39373d789c166a9d516a70591f7ba7b315f3fbf.png)\n\nWhich expression represents the total finite area enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{4}-{x}^{2}$ and $y={x}^{3}-x,$ as shown above?\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=y={x}^{4}-{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)={x}^{3}-x.$ First, we find the intersections of the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{4}-{x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}-x \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-1) & =x({x}^{2}-1) \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area we want consists of two regions: ${A}_{1}$ and ${A}_{2}.$\n\n![](images/d8/d84bd9bccba15a91ab54ee0dc64fb344c191384a14ee5d5c2fb857dc18fc63db.png)\n\nWe have:\n\n- For the area ${A}_{1},$ we have $x\\in [-1,0],$ and the upper function is $g(x)$ while the lower function is $f(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[g(x)-f(x)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}[({x}^{3}-x)-({x}^{4}-{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the area ${A}_{2},$ we have $x\\in [0,1],$ and the upper function is $f(x)$ while the lower function is $g(x).$ So, the area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[f(x)-g(x)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[({x}^{4}-{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}-x)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required area is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f67ba52f-1056-572e-9b04-a2b486e7e734" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532740}}] :activity/title "Finding Areas Between Curves that Intersect at More Than Two Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531931}]}
